/S /H2
/T (p1)
/E (p1)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
350 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (New Zealand climate)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
351 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (New Zealand experiences a diverse range of climates due to its unique geography, which spans from north to south and encompasses mountains, plains, and coastal areas. The country's climate can be broadly categorized into four main types:)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
352 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Subtropical: The northern regions of New Zealand, particularly the Northland and Auckland areas, have a subtropical climate. Summers are warm and humid, while winters are mild, with temperatures rarely dropping below freezing. These regions receive more rainfall throughout the year, and occasional tropical cyclones can affect the area.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
353 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 232 0 R
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
354 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Temperate: Most of the North Island and parts of the northern South Island have a temperate climate. This means warm, dry summers and cooler, wetter winters. Cities like Wellington fall into this category, with comfortable year-round temperatures.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
355 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Continental: The central South Island, including areas like Canterbury and Otago, has a continental climate characterized by hot, dry summers and cold winters with regular frost and occasional snowfall)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
356 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (p2)
/E (p2)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
