653 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Education)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
654 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The education system in New Zealand is known for its high quality and is considered to be one of the best in the world. It is structured in a way that provides a range of options for students at various levels and caters to a diverse range of learning styles and abilities. Below you will find an overview of the key aspects of the New Zealand education system.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
655 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Compulsory Education: Education in New Zealand is compulsory for children aged 6 to 16 years. Most students attend primary and secondary schools during this period.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
656 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Early Childhood Education \(ECE\): New Zealand places a strong emphasis on early childhood education. ECE is available for children from birth to the age of 5 and is not compulsory. It includes a range of options, such as kindergartens, play centres, home-based care, and childcare centers.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
657 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Primary and Secondary Education: The New Zealand education system is divided into primary, intermediate, and secondary schools. Primary school covers Year 1 to Year 8, intermediate covers Year 7 and Year 8, and secondary school covers Year 9 to Year 13. Students typically complete their NCEA \(National Certificate of Educational Achievement\) during these years.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
658 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 257 0 R
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
659 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (National Curriculum: )
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
660 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (New Zealand has a national curriculum that sets out what students are expected to learn at each level of education. The curriculum is designed to provide a broad and balanced education and includes subjects like English, mathematics, science, social studies, physical education, and the arts.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
661 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (p27)
/E (p27)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
662 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Tertiary Education: New Zealand offers a range of tertiary education options, including universities, polytechnics, and institutes of technology. There are eight government-funded universities in the country, offering undergraduate and postgraduate degrees. Polytechnics and institutes of technology focus on vocational and applied learning.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
663 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <>
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
664 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
665 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Special Education: New Zealand has provisions for students with special educational needs. Schools provide support and accommodations for students with disabilities, and there are special education schools and services available.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
666 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (School Governance: Schools in New Zealand have boards of trustees made up of elected parent and community representatives. These boards have significant autonomy in managing school resources and making decisions.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
667 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (School Fees and Funding: Public education in New Zealand is mostly funded by the government, and it is free at the primary and secondary levels. However, parents may be asked to make a voluntary donation to support the school. Tertiary education typically involves tuition fees, although there are scholarships and financial aid options available.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
668 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (How do I enrol my child in a school in New Zealand?)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
669 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Everything you need to enrol your child in a school of your choice can be found in the link below. )
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
